# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THIS REPOSITORY IS PUBLIC. github.com/Danfr4nk/wikitest
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# This file is the UNION of two rule sets, merged per the procedure in the
# old repo's MIGRATION.md: the original wiki-brain-main-1 rules (above) and
# the corpus rules (below). Neither side was dropped. Merging them is the
# whole point — the top half is Dan's decision about Dan, the bottom half is
# a decision about ~498 other people, and they are not the same decision.

# THE INTAKE LEDGER'S DATA IS TRACKED, and this repository is public.
#
# That is a decision, not an oversight, and it was made twice: the guard was
# restored on 2026-08-30 when the "repo is private now" premise turned out to be
# false, and removed again the same day when the operator asked for the ledger to
# sync while the repository is public, stating they understood the implications.
#
# What that means concretely: `intake/events.jsonl` is a public, permanent record
# of consumption, readable by anyone, and git history cannot be un-published. It
# is tracked because the wiki has to be able to read it — a sealed or ignored
# ledger is invisible to the analysis it exists to feed.
#
# To reverse: make the repository private FIRST, verify it, and only then decide
# whether the ignore lines are still wanted. In that order.

# --- corpus rules ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# The message corpus is not Dan's data alone. It contains the phone numbers,
# email addresses and private words of ~498 other people, none of whom chose
# to be published. The default here is closed.
#
# git history cannot be un-published. Un-ignoring any line below puts that
# data on the public internet permanently, for everyone in it.

# Raw corpus — every message, in the clear.
corpus/messages.csv

# Identity material and the hashing salt. The salt is what stops a published
# thread_id from being brute-forced back to a phone number: there are only
# ~10^10 US numbers, so an unsalted hash is not anonymisation, it is a speed
# bump. If this leaks, every derived artifact de-anonymises with it.
corpus/private/

# Shelved fragment exports — superseded, retained locally as error evidence.
#
# Note what these lines do NOT do: the source Google Sheet is deliberately
# shared "anyone with the link", so the corpus is already reachable by URL.
# Ignoring it here keeps it out of git history, which is permanent and
# searchable. That is worth doing on its own terms — but it is not what is
# protecting this data, and it should not be mistaken for that.
shelf/**
!shelf/README.md
!shelf/MANIFEST.md
